  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe forecast remains fairly steady through the workweek ahead\nwith multiple waves of upper level ridging over the Quad State,\nslightly interrupted by a Tuesday impulse which has\nprecipitation further northwest. Surface high pressure is\ndominant over the northeast, and later Mid-Atlantic, keeping\nwinds light and weather conditions dry. Temperatures remain\nabove normal with highs in the upper half of the 70s to near 80,\nand lows in the mid to upper 40s, rising some late week as flow\nbecomes more southerly.\n\nOver the weekend, ridging breaks down and the eastern high\nshifts out to sea, providing a chance of rain. A system tracking\nnortheastward from Mexico into Texas provides weekend rainfall.\nModels differ as to whether this system provides rain Saturday\nor delays into Saturday night-Sunday. While PoPs only peak \naround 40-60% Saturday night, it generally should be considered \nthat there will most likely be some precipitation over the \nweekend at some point, and future runs will help dial in the \ntiming better. Strong troughing early next week out west should \nbring another system to the region early week, with the 6-10 day\nprecipitation outlook leaning above normal for most of the \ncountry (but especially the Desert Southwest). The 6-10 day \ntemperature outlook is strongest over the Ohio Valley. Highs in \nthe 70s and lows in the 50s over the weekend would lend support\nto that, being around 10 degrees above normal.",
